# Packages installed into the live image.
#
# Derived from archiso's `releng` list, with three deliberate changes:
#
#  1. ADDED the desktop: `nidara-desktop` (from the [nidara] repo — see
#     pacman.conf) and the handful of things a live desktop needs that a rescue
#     shell does not. `nidara-desktop` pulls Hyprland, GTK4/GJS, PipeWire,
#     portals, fonts and its own default apps through its depends=() — none of
#     that is listed here.
#  2. SWAPPED the network stack: releng boots iwd + systemd-networkd; Nidara's
#     shell reads NetworkManager's libnm directly, so the live session has to be
#     on NetworkManager or its network panel is blind. This is not a preference.
#  3. REMOVED the rescue-DVD toolbox (clonezilla, partclone, partimage, ddrescue,
#     testdisk, fsarchiver, dmraid, jfsutils, nilfs-utils…), the terminal
#     userland nobody reaches from a desktop (irssi, lynx, mc, screen, tmux,
#     lftp, nmap…) and the VPN/dial-up clients (openvpn, vpnc, openconnect, ppp,
#     pptpclient, xl2tpd, wvdial — NetworkManager plugins cover the modern half).
#     This is an install medium for a desktop, not a maintenance DVD, and every
#     megabyte here is a megabyte of download.
#
# Hardware support is NOT part of that trim: firmware, filesystem tools, wifi
# drivers and VM guest agents stay exactly as releng has them.

# ── base system ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
base
linux
linux-firmware
linux-firmware-marvell
amd-ucode
intel-ucode
mkinitcpio
mkinitcpio-archiso
sof-firmware
sudo
less
nano
man-db
man-pages
terminus-font

# ── the desktop ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Two of the three packages on this list that do not come from Arch's own repos.
# The third is `nidara-installer`, down in "installing", because that is what it
# is — see the note there.
#
# They answer two different questions, which is why they are two packages and
# not one. `nidara-desktop` is what the desktop needs to RUN — without it there
# is no shell. `nidara-apps` is what makes a freshly installed machine USABLE: a
# browser, and whatever else that set turns out to be. Its contents are
# deliberately undecided and live in nidara-repo, one line away from changing;
# what matters here is that the ISO names both and `install.sh` names only the
# first (it runs on an Arch someone already uses, with their own apps chosen).
#
# Everything BELOW this section is the medium's own: kernel, firmware, installer
# tools, VM guest agents. None of it belongs on the machine this ISO installs —
# that system gets `nidara-desktop` + `nidara-apps` and nothing else from here.
# Keeping the two apart is why the browser is no longer a line in this file.
#
# ⚠️ These two, plus `nidara-installer` below, are the only lines here that come
# from a repository WE publish — which makes them the only ones that can be
# missing for a reason other than a typo: `nidara-repo` serves whatever release
# `pins.env` points at, so a package that exists in `nidara-desktop`'s tree but
# not in the pinned tag is simply not there. `build.sh` checks for exactly that
# before mkarchiso does, because "target not found" does not say it.
#
# ⚠️ The desktop package is `nidara-desktop`, NOT `nidara`. It was renamed in
# nidara-desktop v0.10.0 so that the bare name stays free for a future
# metapackage; the two are not interchangeable and `nidara` resolves to nothing.
nidara-desktop
nidara-apps

# ── installing ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `nidara-installer` is the GTK front-end the live session actually opens; it is
# HERE and not in the desktop section on purpose. It is the medium's own tool,
# like `archinstall` two lines down, and the one package of this whole file that
# must never reach the machine being installed — a system somebody is using must
# not carry a program whose job is to erase a disk. That is the entire reason
# the desktop repository's PKGBUILD is a split package: `nidara-desktop` and
# `nidara-installer` are built together and shipped apart, and this file is the
# only place in the world that names the second.
nidara-installer
arch-install-scripts
archinstall
btrfs-progs
cryptsetup
dosfstools
e2fsprogs
efibootmgr
exfatprogs
f2fs-tools
gptfdisk
grub
lvm2
mdadm
mtools
nvme-cli
ntfsprogs
parted
reflector
squashfs-tools
syslinux
xfsprogs

# ── network ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
networkmanager
modemmanager
usb_modeswitch
wpa_supplicant
wireless-regdb
wireless_tools
iw
b43-fwcutter
# ⚠️ `broadcom-wl` USED to be here and is gone from Arch — only `broadcom-wl-dkms`
# remains, and archiso's own releng profile dropped the name too (checked against
# archiso 90). It is not a typo and not an outage: the precompiled module stopped
# being published, so the build fails with `target not found` at pacstrap, after
# setup, which reads exactly like a misspelling.
#
# Not replaced with `broadcom-wl-dkms` on purpose. That would put a COMPILER in
# the live image — dkms depends on gcc, make, patch and binutils, and needs
# `linux-headers` (310 MiB installed) to build against the image's kernel — and
# would make every build depend on a module compile succeeding inside pacstrap.
# Half a gigabyte and a new failure mode for one legacy Broadcom family, on an
# image whose ceiling is already a concern. The official Arch ISO ships no `wl`
# driver either; `b43-fwcutter` above still covers the cards the in-tree drivers
# handle. Revisit only if somebody actually cannot install because of it.
openssh
rsync
bind
systemd-resolvconf

# ── hardware / storage ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
bolt
dmidecode
ethtool
hdparm
libfido2
libusb-compat
lsscsi
mmc-utils
pcsclite
sdparm
sg3_utils
smartmontools
tpm2-tools
tpm2-tss
usbutils
usbmuxd
alsa-utils

# ── virtual machines (this image is tested in one) ───────────────────────────
qemu-guest-agent
open-vm-tools
virtualbox-guest-utils-nox
hyperv
